  "text": "Question seven. What is learning curve analysis? Train on more and more data, and track both errors. A straight line on the sine wave: the two curves meet early and stay high. That's bias, and more data won't help. The degree-nine polynomial: the gap starts wide and closes as the data grows, down to the noise. That's variance, and more data is the cure. Now, scoring a classifier, on a rare event.\n\nQuestion eight. Precision versus recall: when do you favor each? Precision is the share of flagged cases that are real. Recall is the share of real cases you flag. This model scores two hundred thousand cases, two percent of them positive. At a threshold of point eight, precision is eighty-seven percent and recall ten. Lower it to point oh five, and recall climbs to seventy-five while precision falls to eighteen. Favor recall when a miss is costly, like a disease screen or fraud. Favor precision when a false alarm is, like a spam filter. F one, the harmonic mean of the two, balances them.",
